**Read:** [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), environmental conditions, apparatus and flow calibration. Revisit [Relaxation time and the distance before stopping](../particles/Relaxation-time-and-the-distance-before-stopping.lingua#r_69TCZNTFWEQS1C3QEFDW0R5RJ1) for the relation between inertia and a changing gas velocity.

The gas streamlines entering a probe need not carry a representative fraction of every particle size. Inertia, gravity, probe orientation, nearby surfaces and local airflow affect entry. The aspiration ratio compares concentration entering the inlet with the defined surrounding concentration; it is distinct from losses after entry.

For an aligned thin-wall inlet in a uniform stream, matching inlet velocity Q/Ain to surrounding velocity is the isokinetic condition. It reduces a particular velocity mismatch; it does not establish representative sampling in a disturbed or spatially variable region. Large inertial particles and small flow-following particles respond differently to mismatched sampling.

**Example:** An inlet area of 10 mm² in a 0.5 m/s stream requires Q = 5×10⁻⁶ m³/s, or 0.3 L/min, to match mean velocities. A counter drawing 1 L/min through that opening has an inlet speed of about 1.667 m/s. These values identify the mismatch; they do not alone determine its particle bias.

**Work:** Draw the streamtube and limiting trajectories for particles following the gas and particles maintaining their incoming velocity. Explain why sampling one convenient point near a supply outlet can differ from observing a work surface. Propose a comparison that changes inlet position or orientation while holding instrument response fixed. Preserve the geometry and operating state with the resulting data.

